1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,144
Grafana shows gate p95 climbing. Which
span is guilty — gateway auth, gate’s
releaseGate, Feign to billing, or
billing’s SQL?

2
00:00:09,323 --> 00:00:19,067
OpenTelemetry (OTel) standardizes traces,
metrics, and baggage so one check-in
becomes a tree of spans you can open
beside the Prometheus series that alerted
you.

3
00:00:19,187 --> 00:00:34,379
In Spring Boot 3, Micrometer Tracing
bridges to OTel (or Brave). Auto-
instrumentation covers inbound HTTP and
outbound clients. You export via OTLP to a
collector, which fans out to your trace
backend.

4
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:43,067
Correlate by keeping stable metric names
and span names around the same operation —
harbor.gate.release as both timer and span
name is deliberate.

5
00:00:43,188 --> 00:00:57,516
When names diverge (releaseGate in
metrics, GateReleaseService.accept in
traces), humans waste minutes joining
worlds by timestamp alone. Look at this
example. # gate-service Look at this java
for a moment.

6
00:00:57,636 --> 00:01:10,524
Observation is Boot’s preferred API: one
abstraction can emit a timer and a span
together when configured.
lowCardinalityKeyValue mirrors the
Micrometer tag rule. Follow a check-in.

7
00:01:10,644 --> 00:01:22,260
Gateway span (sampled) carries traceparent
to gate. Gate’s Observation creates
harbor.gate.release and nests the Feign
client span. Billing continues the trace.

8
00:01:22,380 --> 00:01:34,524
In the UI you jump from a Grafana spike at
14:02 to traces in that window and sort by
duration. When the breaker opens, child
spans fail fast — the trace shows
milliseconds, not a mystery hang.

9
00:01:34,644 --> 00:01:47,219
Exemplars (when enabled) can link a
Prometheus histogram bucket back to a
trace id; even without them, matching
timestamps and operation names gets you
close. Sampling and cardinality rules
return.

10
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:56,028
100% traces on the public gateway can
drown the collector — symptom: rising
export queue, dropped spans exactly when
incidents happen.

11
00:01:56,147 --> 00:02:08,963
High-cardinality span attributes — raw
plate numbers — create the same cost
problem as metric tags. Put plates in
application logs keyed by trace id
instead. Walk a missing-child failure.

12
00:02:09,084 --> 00:02:24,420
Gate spans exist; billing never appears.
Causes: Feign client not using the
instrumented factory; billing sampling at
zero while gate samples; W3C vs B3
mismatch across a legacy hop; or a sidecar
that strips traceparent.

13
00:02:24,539 --> 00:02:36,323
Fix by curling headers on a test check-in
and confirming the outbound request
carries the parent. Another failure:
custom spans never end() in a finally path
— leaks and skewed durations.

14
00:02:36,443 --> 00:02:48,491
Trade-offs: the Java agent auto-
instruments wide; Micrometer Observation
gives explicit business spans with less
surprise. Use both carefully — double
instrumentation duplicates spans.

15
00:02:48,611 --> 00:02:57,659
Traces without RED metrics leave you blind
between sampled requests; metrics without
traces leave you blind on critical path.
Keep both.

16
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:07,307
When correlating from Grafana, filter
traces by service gate-service, operation
harbor.gate.release, and the spike’s time
window; sort by duration descending.

17
00:03:07,427 --> 00:03:15,060
The top trace usually names the guilty
child — billing SQL, DNS, or a retry storm
— faster than reading three log files.

18
00:03:15,180 --> 00:03:21,396
If no traces appear in the window, check
sampling and collector health before
blaming the application.

19
00:03:21,516 --> 00:03:34,620
A misconception is installing an OTel Java
agent and never verifying Feign/WebClient
propagation on the gate↔billing hop.
Another is treating traces as a
replacement for RED metrics — you need
both.

20
00:03:34,740 --> 00:03:42,228
A third is custom spans around every
getter until the waterfall is unreadable.
You can see where time goes.

21
00:03:42,348 --> 00:03:50,460
The next discipline is deciding what to
change when evidence says a method is hot
or a pool is saturated — tuning from
signals, not folklore.
